//! tr kodlu 1
// 41 — Debugging exercise: following a program step by step with the VS Code debugger.
// What it teaches: setting a breakpoint, opening variables as a table, stepping into a function and changing a value
// while running (for the breakpoint statement in code see 40).
// Builds on: debugging (40), functions (09), lists of dicts (12).
// Run (from the project root): tr examples/en/41_debugging_exercise.tr   (F5 in VS Code)
//
// 1. Click to the left of line 26 to set a breakpoint, then press F5.
// 2. Open the 'orders' variable in the Variables panel; right-click → "TR: Table View".
// 3. Step line by line with F10, step into the 'add_vat' function with F11.
// 4. Double-click the 'rate' variable and type 0.20: the result changes at once.

A030 rate = 0.18
A030 orders = [
    {"customer": "Ayse", "product": "Laptop", "amount": 45000},
    {"customer": "Mehmet", "product": "Phone", "amount": 28000},
    {"customer": "Elif", "product": "Headphones", "amount": 3500}
]

A020 add_vat(amount) {
    A030 vat = amount * rate
    A021 amount + vat
}

A030 grand_total = 0
A010 o A011 orders {
    A030 with_vat = add_vat(o["amount"])
    grand_total += with_vat
    G100("{o["customer"]}: {with_vat} TL")
}

G100("Grand total: {grand_total} TL")

// --- Try it yourself ---
// 1. Add the grand_total expression to the watch panel and follow how it changes every round.
// 2. Give the breakpoint a condition: stop only when amount > 40000.
